            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29Cat"} (∞,1)Cat
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            There are a number of different ways to make the idea of an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-categories"} quasi-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicially
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topologically+enriched+categories"} topologically enriched categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+categories"} Segal categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Segal+spaces"} complete Segal spaces
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity+categories"} categories
            (most of which can be done either simplicially or topologically). Additionally, any notion of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-category"} ∞-category
            can be specialized to a notion of an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\gt 1
            to be invertible.
          %p
            Unlike the case for general notions of an
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            that are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-category
            for a summary of the state of the art about definitions of
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h3#quasicategories Quasi-categories
          %p
            We start with the definition of an “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            \-category” that was promoted by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Andre Joyal
            as a good model for the theory. This goes back to Boardman-Vogt in the 1970s and was further developed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Marc+Cordier"} Jean-Marc Cordier
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"} Tim Porter
            in the early 1980s.
          %p
            This is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+definition+of+higher+category"} geometric definition of higher category
            which conceives of an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            with extra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} property
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
          %p
            Recall that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            in which every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} horn
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^k[n]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi k
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq k \leq n
            has a
            = succeed "." do
              %em filler
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-cell," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em invertibility
            of every cell: if the orientation does not match, we can invert the cell and then compose.
          %p
            From this perspective one observes, by looking closely at the combinatorics, that the invertibility of the 1-cells in the simplicial set is enforced particularly by the condition that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} outer horns
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^0[n]
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^n[n]
            have fillers.
          %p
            Therefore, in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            in which
            %em only the inner horns
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^k[n]
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi k
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \lt k \lt n
            have fillers, all cells are required to have a kind of inverse, except the 1-cells. (They may have inverses, too, but are not required to).
          %p
            This is evidently a realization of the idea of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-category"} (n,r)-category
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = \infty
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r = 1
          %p Such a simplicial set with fillers for all inner horns
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Boardman and Vogt called a
                %em weak Kan complex
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Andre Joyal
                called a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
                called an
                = succeed "-category." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            Here we follow Joyal and say
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
            when we mean concretely the simplicial sets with an extra property. We use the more general term “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            \-category” for this or any of its equivalent models, discussed below, in order to distinguish from the term
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-category"} ∞-category
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-category"} ∞-category
            that is more traditionally understood to generically mean an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-categories"} (n,r)-categories
            = succeed "-category)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,\infty)
          %p
            With
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-categories
            being just
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            with an extra property, there are evident and simple definitions of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category+of+%28infinity%2C1%29-functors"} quasi-category of (∞,1)-functors
                between two
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-categories"} quasi-categories
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %li
              %p
                the quasi-category of all
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category+of+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} quasi-category of all quasi-categories
          %p
            Similarly,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Andre Joyal
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
            have shown that all other constructions in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            have good generalizations to quasi-categories, which usually have conceptually simple formulations: see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
            for more.
          %h3#__and_simplicially_enriched_categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            \-,
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Kan
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Kan
          %p
            Despite the conceptual simplicity of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-categories
          %p
            Recall that we said at the beginning that an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            which is enriched over the category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            As discussed at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model categories
            of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} standard model structure
                on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nice+category+of+spaces"} nice category
                of compactly generated weakly Hausdorff
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} standard model structure
                on the category of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complexes
          %p In fact, this is also equivalent to
          %ul
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} standard model structure
              on the category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SimpSet"} SSet
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
          %p
            If we take the notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            to be the most manifest incarnation of the idea of an “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"}> ∞-groupoid
            ”, then under these equivalences one may think of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
                as representing the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
                which is obtained from it by “freely throwing in the missing inverses” of cells (technically: as representing its fibrant replacement);
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                as representing the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Π
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X)
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    0-cells are the points of
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %li
                  %p
                    1-cells are the paths in
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %li
                  %p
                    2-cells are the triangles in
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %li
                  %p etc.
          %p
            With this interpretation understood (i.e., with these model structures understood),
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SimpSet"} SSet
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p For more, see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+quasi-categories+and+simplicial+categories"} relation between quasi-categories and simplicial categories
          %h3#homotopical_categories Homotopical categories
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical category
            is a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            equipped with a class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,W)
            has a
            %em quasi-localisation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C[W(-1)]
            which is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C[W(-1)]
            is obtained from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            by freely gluing a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            inverse to each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C[W(-1)]
            is equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dwyer-Kan+localisation"} Dwyer-Kan localisation
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with respect to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-categories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicially+enriched+category"} simplicial categories
            mentioned above.
          %p
            Conversely, every quasi-category is equivalent to the quasi-localisation of a homotopical category. This gives a representation of all
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical categories
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %p When the homotopical category (C,W) is obtained from a Quillen model structure (by forgetting the cofibrations and the fibrations) the quasi-category C[W^(-1)] has finite limits and colimits. Conversely, I conjecture that every quasi-category with finite limits and colimits is equivalent to the quasi-localisation of a model category. In fact, every locally presentable quasi-category is a quasi-localisation of a combinatorial model by a result of Lurie. More can be said: the underlying category can taken to be a category of presheaves by a result of Daniel Dugger.
          %p http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0007070
          %h3#model_categories Model categories
          %p
            A specific notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical category
            is that of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally presentable (∞,1)-categories
            are precisely those presented this way by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+category"} combinatorial model categeories
          %p
            At the very beginning, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            was understood as a “model for the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            of topological spaces,” or more precisely
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with extra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} structure and properties
            which allows one to perform all the familiar operations from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
          %p
            As mentioned above, from the point of view of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            may naturally be regarded an as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            and is in fact the archetypical example, analogous to how
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            is the archetypical example of an ordinary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          %p
            This indicates that, more generally, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            should actually be a means to model (i.e., encode) in 1-categorical terms an
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            presents an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{A}
                is, in particular, a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicially+enriched+category"} simplicially enriched category
            %li
              %p
                the full
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SSet"} SSet
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi A
                        %mo ∘
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{A}^\circ
                on the fibrant-cofibrant objects of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{A}
                happens to be
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+coherent+nerve"} homotopy coherent nerve
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi A
                        %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(\mathbf{A}^\circ)
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi A
                        %mo ∘
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{A}^\circ
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
                %em presented
                by
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            Up to equivalence, this gives the same
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicially+enriched+categories"} simplicially enriched categories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-categories
            via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+coherent+nerve"} homotopy coherent nerve
            understood, we shall often not distinguish between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi A
                    %mo ∘
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{A}^\circ
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi A
                    %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(\mathbf{A}^\circ)
            as the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} presented
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h3#segal_categories_and_complete_segal_spaces Segal categories and complete Segal spaces
          %p
            Other models for
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+categories"}> Segal categories
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+Segal+spaces"}> complete Segal spaces
                \.
          %p
            Segal categories can be thought of as categories which are
            %em weakly
            enriched in topological spaces/simplicial sets/Kan complexes, where the definition of “weak” makes use of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SimpSet"} SSet
          %p
            Complete Segal spaces are like
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} internal categories in an (∞,1)-category
          %p
            This construction principle in particular lends itself to iteration and hence to an inductive definition of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-category
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+n-categories"} Segal n-categories
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-fold+complete+Segal+spaces"} n-fold complete Segal spaces
          %h3#categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            \-categories
          %p
            An
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity+category"} category
            can also be thought of as a category “weakly enriched” in spaces (i.e.
            = succeed "-groupoids)," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+definition+of+higher+category"} algebraically
            and parametrized by an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Trimble+n-category"} Trimble
            definition of
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#category_theory_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            \-Category theory
          %p
            A crucial point about the notion of an
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              \-category
            is that it supports all the standard constructions and theorems of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            becomes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
          %h3#the_collection_of_all_categories
            The collection of all
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            The collection of all
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C2%29-category"} (∞,2)-category
            called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29Cat"} (∞,1)Cat
          %p
            Often it is useful to regard that as a (large)
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
          %h3#model_category_presentations Model category presentations
          %p
            There is a wealth of different presentations of
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/table+-+models+for+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} table - models for (∞,1)-categories
          %h2#a_modelindependent_approach A model-independent approach
          %p
            In practice, it can be useful to be able to treat all “presentations of
            = succeed "-categories\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %em within
            any model of
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Consider the
                = succeed "-enriched" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Kan
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Kan
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mi QCat
                        %mo ̲
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{QCat}
                of quasi-categories; for quasi-categories
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %munder
                            %mi hom
                            %mo ̲
                          %munder
                            %mi QCat
                            %mo ̲
                        %mo =
                        %mi ι
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %munder
                            %mi hom
                            %mo ̲
                          %mi sSet
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo ,
                        %mi D
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{hom}_{\underline{QCat}} = \iota(\underline{hom}_{sSet}(C,D))
            %li
              %p
                Define a
                %em relative quasi-category
                to be a quasi-category equipped with a wide sub-quasi-category of “weak equivalences” containing all equivalences. For relative quasi-categories
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,W_C)
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi D
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi W
                          %mi D
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (D,W_D)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %munder
                          %mi hom
                          %mo ̲
                        %munder
                          %mi RelQCat
                          %mo ̲
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi D
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊂
                      %msub
                        %munder
                          %mi hom
                          %mo ̲
                        %munder
                          %mi QCat
                          %mo ̲
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{hom}_{\underline{RelQCat}}((C,W_C),(D,W_D)) \subset \underline{hom}_{\underline{QCat}}(C,D)
                for the sub-Kan complex consisting of those maps which take
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W_C
                into
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi W
                          %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W_D
            %li
              %p
                There is an evident inclusion
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi min
                        %mo :
                        %munder
                          %mi QCat
                          %mo ̲
                        %mo →
                        %munder
                          %mi RelQCat
                          %mo ̲
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} min : \underline{QCat} \to \underline{RelQCat}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                to the relative quasi-category
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo ,
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mo ≃
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,C^\simeq)
            %li
              %p
                Although a Quillen equivalence
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ⇄
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_1 \rightleftarrows M_2
                between model categories determines an equivalence of homotopy categories, note that neither adjoint functor need preserve weak equivalences. On the other hand, the restrictions
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi M
                        %mn 1
                        %mi c
                      %mo ↪
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_1^c \hookrightarrow M_1 \rightarrow M_2
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ←
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ↩
                      %msubsup
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                        %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_1 \leftarrow M_2 \hookleftarrow M_2^f
                (to the cofibrant objects of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_1
                and the fibrant objects of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi M
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_2
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://nyjm.albany.edu/j/2016/22-4.html"} MazelGee16, Figure 1
            %li
              %p
                Using the previous observation, expand the diagram in the introduction of
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1112.0040"} BarwickSchommerPries
                (relating a great many Quillen equivalent model categories presenting “the homotopy theory of
                = succeed "-categories\u201D)" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi K
                      %mo →
                      %munder
                        %mi RelQCat
                        %mo ̲
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : K \to \underline{RelQCat}
                among fibrant objects of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi Cat
                          %mi sSet
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi Bergner
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Cat_{sSet})_{Bergner}
            %li
              %p
                Now, apply the right Quillen equivalence
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi N
                        %mi hc
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi Cat
                        %mi sSet
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi Bergner
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi sSet
                        %mi Joyal
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N^{hc} : (Cat_{sSet})_{Bergner} \to sSet_{Joyal}
                (the homotopy-coherent nerve) to this cospan
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %munder
                          %mi QCat
                          %mo ̲
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mi min
                        %munder
                          %mi RelQCat
                          %mo ̲
                        %mo ←
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{QCat} \xrightarrow{min} \underline{RelQCat} \leftarrow K
            %li
              %p
                The morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi N
                        %mi hc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi min
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N^{hc}(min)
                of quasi-categories admits a contractible Kan complex worth of quasi-categorical left adjoints, any of which presents the
                %em localization
                of relative quasi-categories. Choose one, and denote this quasi-categorical adjunction by
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mo :
                        %msup
                          %mi N
                          %mi hc
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %munder
                          %mi RelQCat
                          %mo ̲
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⇄
                        %msup
                          %mi N
                          %mi hc
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %munder
                          %mi QCat
                          %mo ̲
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %mi min
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L : N^{hc} (\underline{RelQCat}) \rightleftarrows N^{hc} ( \underline{QCat}) : min
            %li
              %p
                It follows from the main theorem of
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0409598"} Toen
                that the composite map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo ∘
                      %msup
                        %mi N
                        %mi hc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %msup
                        %mi N
                        %mi hc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi N
                        %mi hc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %munder
                        %mi RelQCat
                        %mo ̲
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi N
                        %mi hc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %munder
                        %mi QCat
                        %mo ̲
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \circ N^{hc}(F) : N(K) \cong N^{hc}(K) \to N^{hc}(\underline{RelQCat}) \to N^{hc}(\underline{QCat})
                is “essentially contractible” in the quasi-categorical sense. More precisely, for any cofibration into an acyclic object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi K
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ≈
                      %mi pt
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : N(K) \to K' \approx pt
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi sSet
                          %mi Joyal
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet_{Joyal}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo ∘
                      %msup
                        %mi N
                        %mi hc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \circ N^{hc}(F)
                over
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %li
              %p
                Define
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi The
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Cats
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊂
                      %msup
                        %mi N
                        %mi hc
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %munder
                        %mi QCat
                        %mo ̲
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (The(\infty,1)Cats) \subset N^{hc}(\underline{QCat})
                to be the maximal sub-Kan complex generated by the image of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mo ∘
                        %msup
                          %mi N
                          %mi hc
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \circ N^{hc}(F)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Cat
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn ∞
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi The
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Cats
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{(\infty,1)} \in (The(\infty,1)Cats)
                for
                %em any
                vertex, and propose that to work “model-independently” is to work
                %em within
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Cat
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn ∞
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{(\infty,1)}
          %p
            This sequence of maneuvers balances twin aims. On the one hand, Toen’s theorem asserts that after choosing a basepoint, this Kan complex is a model for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathbb{Z}/2)
            = succeed "-category\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{(\infty,1)}
            (where “canonical” must still be taken in the quasi-categorical sense). On the other hand, it is completely independent of which vertex of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi The
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Cats
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (The(\infty,1)Cats)
            we choose.
          %p
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/relcats-modelcats-qcats-inftycats.jpg"}> This diagram
            , taking place in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi N
                      %mi hc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mi QCat
                      %mo ̲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N^{hc}(\underline{QCat})
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/7029/a-diagram-relating-different-models-of-inftycategories/#Item_0"} here
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/table+-+models+for+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} table - models for (∞,1)-categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-category"}> 0-category
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-category"} (0,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"}> 2-category
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-category"} (2,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-category"} 3-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C0%29-category"} (∞,0)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2C1%29-category"} (n,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %strong> (∞,1)-category
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} internal (∞,1)-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally cartesian closed (∞,1)-category
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiadditive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"}> semiadditive (∞,1)-category
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} additive (∞,1)-category
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} monoidal (∞,1)-category
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"}> locally presentable (∞,1)-category
                    ,
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} accessible (∞,1)-category
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} compactly generated (∞,1)-category
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunctive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} disjunctive (∞,1)-category
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} model (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C2%29-category"} (∞,2)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-category"} (n,r)-category
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            For several years,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"} André Joyal
            – who was one of the first to promote the idea that for studying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            it is good to first study
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-categories
            – has been preparing a textbook on the subject. This still doesn’t quite exist, but an extensive write-up of lecture notes does:
          %ul
            %li#Joyal08
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Theory+of+Quasi-Categories+and+its+Applications"} The Theory of Quasi-Categories and its Applications
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://lists.lehigh.edu/pipermail/algtop-l/2007q4/000017.html"} Advanced Course on Simplicial Methods in Higher Categories
              %strong 45
              2, Centre de Recerca Matemàtica, Barcelona (2008) &amp;lbrack;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/JoyalTheoryOfQuasiCategories.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Further notes (where the term
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logos"} logos
            is used instead of
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
          %ul
            %li#Joyal08
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
              \:
              %em Notes on Logoi
              (2008) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.uchicago.edu/~may/IMA/JOYAL/Joyal.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/JoyalOnLogoi2008.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Meanwhile
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicially+enriched+category"} simplicially enriched category
            is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 170
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://press.princeton.edu/titles/8957.html"} pup:8957
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.math.ias.edu/~lurie/papers/HTT.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            A brief exposition from the point of view of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            is in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              = succeed "," do
